---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { AUTHOR, SITE_TITLE, SOCIALS } from '../consts';
import { breadcrumbSchema, personSchema } from '../lib/seo';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`About — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description={`${AUTHOR}: a backend engineer moving from B2B billing systems into GPU inference infrastructure. Why this site exists and what gets written here.`}
			schema={[
				{ '@type': 'ProfilePage', mainEntity: personSchema(Astro.site) },
				breadcrumbSchema(Astro.site, [
					{ name: SITE_TITLE, path: '/' },
					{ name: 'About', path: '/about/' },
				]),
			]}
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main class="shell shell--narrow">
			<div class="page-head">
				<p class="label">About</p>
				<h1>Learning inference infrastructure the slow way</h1>
			</div>

			<div class="prose body">
				<p>
					I'm {AUTHOR}. For the past several years I've built B2B SaaS billing systems — subscription
					lifecycles, proration, invoice generation, the kind of software where a rounding error
					shows up on someone's bank statement. It taught me to chase a number until it reconciles.
				</p>
				<p>
					I'm now pointing that habit at GPUs. This site is the working log: what I measure, what I
					got wrong, and what the arithmetic actually says. Not tutorials — I'm not far enough along
					to teach anyone. Closer to lab notes that happen to be public.
				</p>

				<h2>What gets written here</h2>
				<p>The plan, roughly in order:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						<strong>Reading the hardware.</strong> Roofline analysis, arithmetic intensity, and using
						a profiler to tell memory stalls from launch overhead.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Kernels.</strong> Triton, then the fused patterns that matter for attention and
						normalisation.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Engines.</strong> Building a minimal inference engine end to end — KV cache,
						batching, scheduling — then reading vLLM to find out how wrong I was.
					</li>
					<li>
						<strong>Scale.</strong> Sharding, and what actually happens when a node fails mid-run.
					</li>
				</ul>

				<h2>House rules</h2>
				<p>
					Every performance claim comes with the hardware, the shapes, and how it was measured. If a
					number is from a spec sheet rather than my own run, it says so. Posts get corrected in
					place, with the change noted at the bottom — I'd rather be usefully wrong in public than
					quietly vague.
				</p>

				<h2>Elsewhere</h2>
				<p>
					<a href={SOCIALS.github} rel="me noopener" target="_blank">GitHub</a> ·
					<a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>
				</p>
			</div>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.page-head {
		padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem) 1.4rem;
		border-bottom: var(--rule-weight) solid var(--rule);
	}

	.page-head .label {
		margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
	}

	h1 {
		font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4vw, 2.6rem);
		max-width: 20ch;
	}

	.body {
		padding-top: 2.25rem;
	}
</style>
